{
  "image_name": "b9c3270c118acb95ec24ef268dee988c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four ways a liquid droplet can be separated from a solid surface by an intermediate layer, highlighting how different interfaces reduce adhesion and help droplets move.\n\nOne route is a vapour-mediated surface, where a droplet sits on a hot substrate and is lifted by a thin vapour cushion. Another is an air-mediated surface, where rough or structured textures trap air beneath the liquid. Two examples are shown: SHPS, with air pockets held in surface features, and SOPS, which also maintains an air layer under the drop.\n\nThe lower half shows liquid-mediated surfaces. In SLIPS, the solid is infused with a lubricant, so the droplet contacts a smooth liquid film instead of the solid itself. The final concept, labeled LLS, uses a smooth surface coated with an ultrathin liquid-like layer only about 1–10 nm thick. The text emphasizes that this LLS design offers low contact angle hysteresis for many liquids, good interface stability, and simple, scalable fabrication.\n\nOverall, the image organizes anti-wetting strategies by the type of interfacial layer—vapour, air, lubricant, or liquid-like coating—and suggests that minimizing direct liquid–solid contact is the key shared mechanism.",
